/* ================= DIALOGUE =================
   The pets actually talking — to each other, past each other, and straight at you.

   Everything else in this game is third-person narration ("Refused to downsize. The
   pile has tenure."). This file is the other half, and it follows the house comedy
   spec in docs/comedy-direction.md:

     1. THE FUNNY HALF IS THE SECOND SPEAKER. The complainer speaks; the neighbour is
        bored of it. Write toward the straight man, never the performer.
     2. PHYSICAL FACTS ARE LOAD-BEARING. Four inches tall, wrong-textured, glazed,
        cracked, damp, on a grid of squares, and unable to die. If a line would
        survive being said by an adult in a flatshare, it is not a Shelf Life line.
     3. STOP ONE BEAT EARLY. If the last turn explains the joke, it is deleted.
     4. RATION THE WITHHOLD. "Won't say what" is the game's tic. Roughly one in ten.
     5. SHORT. Median around 53 characters. Two to four turns beats a sketch.

   ---- SHAPES ----

   An EXCHANGE is  { turns: [[who, line], ...], night?, mood? }
     `who` is a role key, not a name. engine/dialogue.js resolves it against the
     current shelf and hands the renderer { who, speaker, line } per turn:
       'a' / 'b'   the two adjacent pets in a two-hander
       'c'         a third pet — chorus scenes, and the voice of a reaction shot
       'all'       every pet in the scene, speaking at once
       'p'         the pet doing the talking (direct address / fragments)
       'n'         a neighbour who interrupts (direct address only)
     `night`  restricts the entry to night hours (engine/tick.js isNight).
     `mood`   a mood string or array; fires only if a participant is in that mood.

   A REACTION SHOT is { setup, turns: [['c', line]] } — somebody did a thing, and the
   note is about who watched. The setup is unattributed narration; the only speech is
   the bystander's.

   PLACEHOLDERS, substituted before render:
       {a} {b}     the other pet, named in the third person while standing right
                   there. Also used in a reaction shot's setup.
       {n}         a neighbour. Legal ONLY in NEIGHBOUR_FRAGMENTS and in a direct
                   address entry marked needs:'neighbor'. Anywhere else it renders as
                   literal braces, because nothing substitutes it.
       {p}         unused: the speaker's own name is the attribution. */

